"""LiteLLM custom callback: a reject-fast, per-caller session lock for the
RTX 4080's single llama.cpp slot (guest llm-4080), PLUS a second, independent
gate for the Mac Studio's servable ids (see "THE STUDIO GATE" below) —
different lock keys, so contention on one never blocks the other.

Deployed by roles/llm_router next to config.yaml, and resolved by LiteLLM
from litellm_settings.callbacks: "fast_subagent_lock.proxy_handler_instance"
(config.yaml.j2) — LiteLLM imports a callback module relative to the config
file's own directory, so this file must live beside config.yaml. NOT smoke-
tested against a live proxy in the PR that added this; see the PR body.

Rationale for the lock, the model set it covers, and the TTL:
roles/llm_router/defaults/main/57-subagent-lock.yml.

TWO DIFFERENT BEHAVIORS ON CONTENTION, by how the caller named the request:

  * A direct call (GATED_MODELS — hermes-local-4080, either vllm-tier id, or
    the `fast-gpu` DB role, which is unioned in for exactly this hard-429
    treatment; see 57-subagent-lock.yml for why it must not redirect) gets
    the hard 429 above. Unchanged from the first version of this file.

  * A `fast`/`subagent` role call (ROLE_NAMES) REDIRECTS onto
    ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET instead of rejecting. This is deliberately NOT "raise
    the same 429 and let LiteLLM's Router fall back" — `async_pre_call_hook`
    runs in the PROXY layer (`proxy_logging_obj.pre_call_hook`, called before
    `llm_router.acompletion()`), before the Router's own fallback/cooldown
    engine is ever entered, so an exception raised here propagates straight to
    the client and CANNOT trigger a Router-level fallback, regardless of
    exception type (a `litellm.RateLimitError` would not behave differently
    here than the `HTTPException` above — neither ever reaches the Router).
    This is the author's best understanding of the pinned litellm[proxy]
    architecture, NOT verified against a live process in this PR — confirm
    before trusting it under load. Rewriting `data["model"]` here, before
    dispatch, is the mechanism `async_pre_call_hook` actually supports for
    changing what a request hits.

    ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET is TEMPLATED AT CONVERGE TIME (the chain's own
    second rung — see 55-roles.yml's `_llm_router_fast_subagent_chain`), not
    read live from the role's own DB-stored fallback order. An operator who
    reorders `fast`/`subagent` in the Admin UI changes the ORDINARY (non-
    contention) fallback path immediately — that is plain LiteLLM role
    routing, unaffected by this file — but does NOT change what THIS
    callback redirects to on contention specifically, until the next
    converge re-renders this file. Flagged as a real limitation, not solved
    here: a live lookup would need either a second network hop to this same
    proxy's own management API on every contended request, or an in-process
    read of LiteLLM's own Router object, and neither was verifiable without a
    running proxy.

ACQUISITION HAPPENS BEFORE THE ROUTER PICKS A RUNG, which is a known,
partial limitation, not a solved one. `async_pre_call_hook` fires once per
REQUEST — before litellm has attempted any deployment — so it cannot tell
whether THIS specific attempt will actually reach hermes-local-4080's
deployment (today it always does: hermes-local-4080 is `fast`'s and
`subagent`'s ONLY deployment, so the first attempt always targets it, but
that is a fact about the CURRENT converge, not something this file can
verify at runtime — see the redirect-target limitation above for the same
root cause). What this file DOES do about it: hold the lock for exactly as
long as the underlying work is in flight, not the full TTL. Every
`fast`/`subagent` acquisition auto-releases on that SAME request's own
completion — success, failure, or (the path every real client in this
estate actually uses) an SSE stream finishing or erroring — via
async_post_call_success_hook / async_post_call_failure_hook /
async_post_call_streaming_iterator_hook below, see that method's own
docstring for why the streaming case needs a real override rather than
relying on the two hooks above. So a caller that finishes in 5s frees
the slot in 5s, not 300s, EVEN IF the Router's own ordinary fallback chain
carried the request past hermes-local-4080 to a later rung (Mac Studio /
cloud / long) without this file's involvement — release does not depend on
which rung actually served the request. This does not change WHEN the lock
is taken, only how long it is held, which is what actually matters for
"does an idle 4080 lock out fast-gpu/direct callers" — that window is now
bounded by one request's wall-clock time, not the TTL. A genuine
acquisition-time fix (gate on the Router's actually-resolved deployment, not
the caller-typed name) would need a hook this file could not identify with
confidence.

WHY RELEASE READS data["metadata"], NOT data["model"]. The first version of
this fix keyed release eligibility off `data.get("model")`, matching it
against ROLE_NAMES/GATED_MODELS the same way the pre-call hook does. That
breaks the moment the Router's own fallback machinery rewrites `data["model"]`
to reflect whichever deployment actually served the request (LiteLLM's
documented pattern for request logging) — a `fast` call that fell through to
a cloud rung would then read back as some unrelated model id at post-call
time, `is_role_call` would evaluate false, and `_maybe_release` would skip
entirely: the exact "holds the lock for the full 300s while the 4080 sits
idle" failure this fix exists to prevent, and the one that makes `fast-gpu`'s
429-means-busy signal unreliable for every caller told to trust it. Fixed by
stamping the caller shape ("role" vs "direct") into `data["metadata"]` in
`async_pre_call_hook`, before anything downstream has a chance to touch
`data["model"]`, and reading that stamp back in `_maybe_release` instead.
`metadata` is litellm's own documented pass-through field for exactly this
kind of request-scoped custom data, and unlike `model` nothing else in the
request lifecycle has a reason to overwrite it.

Direct callers (GATED_MODELS) do NOT auto-release on completion — that
would break the ORIGINAL feature this lock exists for (a caller like Hermes
holding the lock across several repeat calls to keep the KV cache warm,
57-subagent-lock.yml). Auto-release is scoped to ROLE_NAMES only, because
`fast`/`subagent` calls are single-shot ad hoc delegation by design (no
multi-call session), so releasing after the one call that used the lock is
correct there and would be wrong for a direct caller.

PARTICIPATES, NOT ACQUIRED — two races a naive "release on every role call's
completion" gets wrong, both real:

  (a) The SAME caller alias holds the lock via a direct `fast-gpu` call
      (deliberate multi-call hold, KV-cache affinity) and separately makes a
      `fast` role call. That role call's acquire attempt finds its own
      caller_id already holding the lock and refreshes it — but the hold
      belongs to the direct call, not to this role call. If this role call
      released on its own completion, it would tear down the direct caller's
      still-active hold out from under it.

  (b) Two role calls from the SAME caller alias run concurrently (parallel
      subagents dispatched from one workstation key). The first to acquire
      establishes the hold; the second refreshes it. If release were keyed
      on "did I ever touch the lock", the FIRST call finishing would delete
      the lock while the SECOND is still mid-flight, letting a third caller
      in on a 4080 slot that is not actually free.

Both are solved together by _ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY, a companion counter whose
mere EXISTENCE (not its value) marks a hold as role-owned. A role call
"participates" (sets `_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY`, and thus owes a release on
its own completion) only when it freshly acquires the lock, or refreshes a
hold the counter shows is ALREADY role-owned — never when it refreshes a
hold with no counter, which can only mean a direct caller (case a)
established it. `_release_role_participant` decrements the counter and
deletes the lock only once it reaches zero (case b) — see
_ACQUIRE_OR_REFRESH_ROLE_SCRIPT / _RELEASE_ROLE_PARTICIPANT_SCRIPT.

DOCUMENTED CEILING, deliberately not closed: the mirror image of case (a).
A role call freshly acquires (participates, role-owned), and a DIRECT
caller (fast-gpu) then refreshes that SAME hold via the ordinary direct-
caller path (_refresh_if_holder — unchanged, never touches the counter).
When the original role call completes, it still owns one share of a
counter now at 1, decrements to 0, and deletes the lock — even though the
direct caller is still relying on it. Symmetric-looking to case (a), but
NOT fixed the same way: a direct caller relies on the 300s TTL or an
explicit X-Subagent-Release, never a completion-triggered auto-release (see
above for why), so there is no signal this file could use to mark "a direct
caller has since taken over this hold" without giving direct callers the
same auto-release-on-completion behavior that was deliberately rejected for
them — reopening the idle-GPU-held-for-the-full-TTL failure for a direct
caller that never sends the header. Accepted as a narrow, understood gap
rather than compounding the design to close it: a role call establishing
the hold, followed by that SAME caller alias pivoting to a direct call
before the role call finishes, is a narrower window than the un-mitigated
"redirect always holds 300s" failure this whole file exists to fix.

Both the refresh-if-same-holder and release-if-same-holder operations use a
Lua script (single Redis round trip: GET-compare-then-EXPIRE/DEL
atomically) instead of separate GET-then-EXPIRE / GET-then-DELETE calls —
the two-call form has a TOCTOU window where a DIFFERENT, newer holder's
lock could be refreshed or deleted by a caller who no longer holds it,
between the GET and the second call.

THE STUDIO GATE. `llm_router_local_max_parallel_requests` / each large-tier
entry's own `max_parallel_requests` (llm-models.d/10-large.yml) is a LiteLLM
Router-internal `asyncio.Semaphore`, acquired with a plain `async with` — no
timeout wraps that acquire (confirmed against litellm==1.102.0 router.py
`_acompletion`, not merely inferred). So a second caller against a busy
studio deployment QUEUES at the Router layer indefinitely, the exact defect
this file exists to avoid for the 4080. STUDIO_GATED_MODELS/STUDIO_ROLE_NAMES/
STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW below give the studio the same reject-fast/redirect-fast
treatment, under a SEPARATE lock key (subagent:studio:lock) so the two
backends never block each other. Two differences from the 4080 gate, both
documented in 57-subagent-lock.yml:

  * ONE shared lock covers all four studio ids (primary/routine/small/ocr),
    even though — unlike the 4080's three ids, which share one physical
    process — primary and routine are two separately resident processes
    that CAN genuinely run concurrently (maxResidentWorkers=2). This is a
    deliberate simplification trading some of that real concurrency for one
    lock to reason about (see 57-subagent-lock.yml for the full tradeoff and
    the upgrade path — split into a per-id lock, or a counting semaphore
    above capacity 1, if it ever measures worse than the wait it replaces).

  * A role call's redirect target is NOT a single constant the way
    ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET is for the 4080 (redirecting to the studio again
    would just move the wait, not remove it). STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW is a
    per-role map to that role's own first NON-LOCAL rung (its declared
    fallback chain, first entry outside both locks' gated sets — see
    57-subagent-lock.yml's derivation). A role absent from the map (no
    non-local rung exists for it) is left to queue on the Router's own
    semaphore — today's pre-existing behaviour, never a hard failure from
    this hook.

Both gates are checked in ONE `async_pre_call_hook` invocation because a
role call's chain can visit BOTH backends (fast/subagent/cheap/review-oss/
review-private hit the 4080 first, then the studio): if the 4080 gate
redirects such a call, `model` is now a studio id and must be checked
against the studio gate before this hook returns — otherwise the request
would sail past the 4080 fix straight into the exact studio-side queuing
this second gate exists to prevent. `judge` skips the 4080 gate entirely
(its target is a studio id directly) and is checked only against the studio
gate. The original role name is preserved as `original_role` across a
possible 4080 redirect specifically so STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW can still be
looked up by the caller's actual role, not by whatever `model` a first-stage
redirect left behind.
"""

import os

from fastapi import HTTPException
from litellm.integrations.custom_logger import CustomLogger

try:
    import redis.asyncio as redis_asyncio
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - always present inside the litellm[proxy] venv
    redis_asyncio = None

LOCK_KEY = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_key | to_json }}
LOCK_TTL_SECONDS = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_ttl_seconds }}
RELEASE_HEADER = {{ llm_router_subagent_lock_release_header | to_json }}
# The client_model_id set that shares the 4080's single llama.cpp slot and KV
# cache — derived from the registry by tier, plus the `fast-gpu` DB role
# name, see 57-subagent-lock.yml.
GATED_MODELS = frozenset({{ llm_router_subagent_lock_model_ids | to_json }})
# DB-seeded role names whose calls redirect around contention instead of
# hard-rejecting — see 55-roles.yml and the module docstring above.
ROLE_NAMES = frozenset({{ llm_router_subagent_lock_role_names | to_json }})
ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET = {{ llm_router_primary_model | to_json }}
# metadata key _maybe_release reads to learn the caller shape (role vs
# direct) that async_pre_call_hook determined — see the module docstring for
# why this cannot be re-derived from data["model"] at post-call time.
_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY = "subagent_lock_caller_shape"
# metadata key: True only when THIS role call actually joined the
# role-tracked hold (either it freshly acquired the lock, or it refreshed a
# hold an earlier role call already joined) — see the module docstring's
# "PARTICIPATES, NOT ACQUIRED" section for why a role call that merely
# refreshed someone else's (a direct caller's) hold must NOT set this.
_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY = "subagent_lock_role_participates"
# Companion key tracking how many in-flight role calls currently share the
# lock — see the module docstring.
_ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY = LOCK_KEY + ":role_inflight"

# --- THE STUDIO GATE (see "THE STUDIO GATE" in the module docstring) ---------
STUDIO_LOCK_KEY = {{ llm_router_studio_lock_key | to_json }}
STUDIO_GATED_MODELS = frozenset({{ llm_router_studio_lock_model_ids | to_json }})
STUDIO_ROLE_NAMES = frozenset({{ llm_router_studio_lock_role_names | to_json }})
# role -> first non-local rung to redirect onto on studio contention; a role
# with no non-local rung is simply absent — see 57-subagent-lock.yml.
STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW = {{ llm_router_studio_role_overflow_targets | to_json }}
_STUDIO_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY = "studio_lock_caller_shape"
_STUDIO_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY = "studio_lock_role_participates"
_STUDIO_ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY = STUDIO_LOCK_KEY + ":role_inflight"

# Same connection this proxy already uses for spend tracking (router_settings
# in config.yaml.j2): host/password via the systemd EnvironmentFile, port as
# a non-secret literal baked in at render time — reusing that pattern rather
# than declaring a second Redis config.
_REDIS_HOST = os.environ.get("LLM_ROUTER_REDIS_HOST", "")
_REDIS_PORT = {{ llm_router_redis_port }}
_REDIS_PASSWORD = os.environ.get("LLM_ROUTER_REDIS_PASSWORD") or None


# Atomic compare-and-refresh: extends the TTL only if the caller asking is
# still the recorded holder. Returns 1 on refresh, 0 if the key is absent or
# held by someone else (never raises for "not the holder" — that is an
# ordinary, expected outcome, not an error).
_REFRESH_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT = """
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[1], ARGV[2])
else
  return 0
end
"""

# Atomic compare-and-delete: same shape, for release instead of refresh.
_RELEASE_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT = """
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  return redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])
else
  return 0
end
"""

# Role-call acquire-or-refresh, single round trip. Distinguishes THREE
# outcomes (KEYS[1]=LOCK_KEY, KEYS[2]=role-inflight counter,
# ARGV[1]=caller_id, ARGV[2]=ttl):
#   2 = fresh acquire, or a refresh of a hold role calls already joined
#       (the counter key exists) — this call now counts toward the
#       inflight total and MUST decrement it on its own completion.
#   1 = refreshed the SAME caller's hold, but the counter key is absent,
#       meaning that hold was established by a direct caller (fast-gpu /
#       hermes-local-4080), not by any role call — proceeds to the
#       deployment same as 2, but must NOT touch the counter on
#       completion: releasing here would delete a direct caller's
#       deliberate multi-call hold out from under it.
#   0 = contended by a different caller — the pre-call hook redirects.
# The counter's own existence (not its value) is what marks a hold as
# role-owned; only a role call's own fresh acquire or a refresh of an
# already role-owned hold creates/grows it.
_ACQUIRE_OR_REFRESH_ROLE_SCRIPT = """
local holder = redis.call("GET", KEYS[1])
if not holder then
  redis.call("SET", KEYS[1], ARGV[1], "EX", ARGV[2])
  redis.call("SET", KEYS[2], 1, "EX", ARGV[2])
  return 2
end
if holder ~= ARGV[1] then
  return 0
end
redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[1], ARGV[2])
if redis.call("EXISTS", KEYS[2]) == 1 then
  redis.call("INCR", KEYS[2])
  redis.call("EXPIRE", KEYS[2], ARGV[2])
  return 2
end
return 1
"""

# Release one role call's share of the inflight count. Deletes the lock (and
# the counter) only once every role call that joined it has released —
# case (b): parallel subagents on one caller must not have the first one to
# finish evict the ones still in flight. The EXISTS guard matters: a long
# role stream plus a direct caller's own refreshes (which only EXPIRE
# LOCK_KEY, never the counter) can let the counter expire and vanish before
# the stream's own release runs — DECR on a missing key returns -1, which
# without this guard would delete a lock this call was never tracked
# against.
_RELEASE_ROLE_PARTICIPANT_SCRIPT = """
if redis.call("GET", KEYS[1]) ~= ARGV[1] then
  return 0
end
if redis.call("EXISTS", KEYS[2]) == 0 then
  return 0
end
local n = redis.call("DECR", KEYS[2])
if n <= 0 then
  redis.call("DEL", KEYS[1])
  redis.call("DEL", KEYS[2])
  return 1
end
return 0
"""


def _caller_id(user_api_key_dict) -> str:
    """The identity a lock decision is made against — a virtual key's alias
    (roles/llm_router/defaults/main/56-virtual-keys.yml), falling back to the
    key's own token for an unaliased/master-key caller so the lock still
    degrades to a real (if less readable) identity rather than colliding
    every unattributed caller onto the literal string "unknown"."""
    return (
        getattr(user_api_key_dict, "key_alias", None)
        or getattr(user_api_key_dict, "token", None)
        or "unknown"
    )


def _release_requested(data: dict) -> bool:
    headers = (data.get("proxy_server_request") or {}).get("headers") or {}
    return str(headers.get(RELEASE_HEADER, "")).strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes")


class FastSubagentLock(CustomLogger):
    """One caller at a time on the 4080. A direct caller (GATED_MODELS) that
    loses the race is rejected with 429 immediately — no wait-queue, no
    preemption; it holds the lock across repeat calls (cache-affinity across
    a session), released only by the 300s TTL or an explicit
    X-Subagent-Release. A fast/subagent role caller (ROLE_NAMES) that loses
    the race is silently redirected instead, and — unlike a direct caller —
    auto-releases its share of the lock when its own call completes
    (success, failure, or its stream ending), but only if it actually
    PARTICIPATES in the hold: see the module docstring's "PARTICIPATES, NOT
    ACQUIRED" section for why a role call that merely refreshed someone
    else's hold must not release it."""

    def __init__(self, client=None):
        # Injectable for tests (tests/llm_router/test_fast_subagent_lock.py);
        # production always lazy-connects via _get_client below.
        self._client = client

    def _get_client(self):
        if self._client is not None:
            return self._client
        if redis_asyncio is None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "redis.asyncio is unavailable — expected inside the litellm[proxy] venv"
            )
        self._client = redis_asyncio.Redis(
            host=_REDIS_HOST,
            port=_REDIS_PORT,
            password=_REDIS_PASSWORD,
            decode_responses=True,
        )
        return self._client

    async def _refresh_if_holder(self, client, lock_key: str, caller_id: str) -> bool:
        return bool(await client.eval(_REFRESH_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT, 1, lock_key, caller_id, LOCK_TTL_SECONDS))

    async def _release_if_holder(self, client, lock_key: str, caller_id: str) -> bool:
        return bool(await client.eval(_RELEASE_IF_HOLDER_SCRIPT, 1, lock_key, caller_id))

    async def _acquire_or_refresh_role(self, client, lock_key: str, inflight_key: str, caller_id: str) -> int:
        return int(
            await client.eval(
                _ACQUIRE_OR_REFRESH_ROLE_SCRIPT, 2, lock_key, inflight_key, caller_id, LOCK_TTL_SECONDS
            )
        )

    async def _release_role_participant(self, client, lock_key: str, inflight_key: str, caller_id: str) -> bool:
        return bool(await client.eval(_RELEASE_ROLE_PARTICIPANT_SCRIPT, 2, lock_key, inflight_key, caller_id))

    async def _reject_direct(self, client, lock_key: str, caller_id: str, backend_name: str) -> None:
        """Shared by both gates' direct-caller path: acquire-or-refresh, or
        raise the hard 429. See the module docstring — this is the "no
        wait-queue, no preemption" half of both locks."""
        acquired = await client.set(lock_key, caller_id, nx=True, ex=LOCK_TTL_SECONDS)
        if acquired:
            return
        if await self._refresh_if_holder(client, lock_key, caller_id):
            return
        holder = await client.get(lock_key)
        raise HTTPException(
            status_code=429,
            detail={
                "error": f"{backend_name} is locked by another caller",
                "held_by": holder,
                "retry_after_seconds": LOCK_TTL_SECONDS,
            },
            headers={"Retry-After": str(LOCK_TTL_SECONDS)},
        )

    async def async_pre_call_hook(self, user_api_key_dict, cache, data: dict, call_type: str):
        model = data.get("model")
        # Preserved across a possible 4080 redirect below — STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW
        # is keyed by the caller's ACTUAL role, not by whatever `model` a
        # first-stage redirect leaves behind. See the module docstring's
        # "Both gates are checked in ONE hook invocation" section.
        original_role = model
        caller_id = _caller_id(user_api_key_dict)
        client = self._get_client()

        # --- Stage 1: the 4080 gate — UNCHANGED behaviour/config ---------------
        is_role_call = model in ROLE_NAMES
        if model in GATED_MODELS or is_role_call:
            # Stamped BEFORE anything downstream (the Router's own fallback
            # machinery included) gets a chance to rewrite data["model"] —
            # see the module docstring. _maybe_release reads this, never
            # data["model"].
            metadata = data.setdefault("metadata", {})
            metadata[_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY] = "role" if is_role_call else "direct"
            if is_role_call:
                outcome = await self._acquire_or_refresh_role(client, LOCK_KEY, _ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY, caller_id)
                if outcome == 0:
                    # Contended by a different caller. See the module
                    # docstring: redirect, not raise — an exception here
                    # cannot reach the Router's own fallback engine. Falls
                    # through to Stage 2 below: the redirect target IS a
                    # studio id and must be checked against that gate too.
                    data["model"] = ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET
                    model = ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET
                else:
                    if outcome == 2:
                        # This call now shares the role-tracked hold and
                        # must release its share on completion — see
                        # _maybe_release and the module docstring's
                        # "PARTICIPATES, NOT ACQUIRED" section for why
                        # outcome == 1 (refreshed a direct caller's hold)
                        # deliberately does NOT set this.
                        metadata[_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY] = True
                    # Reached the 4080 cleanly — no need to also check the
                    # studio gate for this attempt.
                    return data
            else:
                # Direct caller of a GATED_MODELS id: hard 429, unchanged.
                await self._reject_direct(client, LOCK_KEY, caller_id, "llm-4080")
                return data

        # --- Stage 2: the studio gate — see "THE STUDIO GATE" in the module ----
        # docstring. Reached either directly (a caller named a studio id or
        # `judge`, which never touches the 4080) or via the 4080 redirect
        # above (model is now ROLE_REDIRECT_TARGET, a studio id).
        is_studio_role = original_role in STUDIO_ROLE_NAMES
        if model not in STUDIO_GATED_MODELS and not is_studio_role:
            return data
        metadata = data.setdefault("metadata", {})
        metadata[_STUDIO_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY] = "role" if is_studio_role else "direct"
        if is_studio_role:
            outcome = await self._acquire_or_refresh_role(
                client, STUDIO_LOCK_KEY, _STUDIO_ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY, caller_id
            )
            if outcome == 0:
                overflow = STUDIO_ROLE_OVERFLOW.get(original_role)
                if overflow is not None:
                    data["model"] = overflow
                # else: no non-local rung declared for this role — queue on
                # the Router's own semaphore, today's pre-existing
                # behaviour, rather than raise (this hook never hard-fails
                # a role call — see 57-subagent-lock.yml).
                return data
            if outcome == 2:
                metadata[_STUDIO_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY] = True
            return data
        await self._reject_direct(client, STUDIO_LOCK_KEY, caller_id, "llm-large")
        return data

    async def _maybe_release(self, data: dict, user_api_key_dict) -> None:
        """Shared by every post-call hook (success, failure, and the
        streaming iterator below) — see the module docstring for why role
        calls auto-release unconditionally while direct calls release only
        on the explicit header, and for why this reads data["metadata"]
        rather than data["model"]. Releases BOTH gates independently: a
        request that participated in the studio hold, the 4080 hold, or
        both (impossible today — Stage 1 returns before Stage 2 runs
        whenever the 4080 itself served the request — but a role call
        redirected onto the studio only ever holds the studio's share) is
        released once per gate whose shape metadata is present."""
        metadata = data.get("metadata") or {}
        caller_id = _caller_id(user_api_key_dict)
        client = self._get_client()
        shape = metadata.get(_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY)
        if shape == "role":
            # pop, not get: an upstream exception mid-stream reaches BOTH
            # the streaming iterator hook's `finally` and, afterwards,
            # async_post_call_failure_hook on the SAME request_data dict —
            # litellm's own generator wrapper re-raises into its except
            # block, which calls the failure hook after the iterator hook's
            # finally has already run. Without consuming the flag here, that
            # second call would see it still set and decrement the inflight
            # counter a second time for one call, stealing a share that
            # belongs to a genuinely separate, still in-flight sibling.
            if metadata.pop(_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY, None):
                await self._release_role_participant(client, LOCK_KEY, _ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY, caller_id)
        elif shape == "direct" and _release_requested(data):
            await self._release_if_holder(client, LOCK_KEY, caller_id)
        studio_shape = metadata.get(_STUDIO_LOCK_SHAPE_KEY)
        if studio_shape == "role":
            if metadata.pop(_STUDIO_LOCK_PARTICIPATES_KEY, None):
                await self._release_role_participant(client, STUDIO_LOCK_KEY, _STUDIO_ROLE_INFLIGHT_KEY, caller_id)
        elif studio_shape == "direct" and _release_requested(data):
            await self._release_if_holder(client, STUDIO_LOCK_KEY, caller_id)

    async def async_post_call_success_hook(self, data: dict, user_api_key_dict, response):
        await self._maybe_release(data, user_api_key_dict)
        return response

    async def async_post_call_streaming_iterator_hook(self, user_api_key_dict, response, request_data: dict):
        """The non-streaming success hook above is NEVER reached for a
        streaming request on the pinned litellm[proxy]==1.98.0 — the proxy's
        base request processor returns the SSE generator straight to the
        client via its own select_data_generator branch before it ever calls
        proxy_logging_obj.post_call_success_hook (litellm/proxy/
        common_request_processing.py). Every real client in this estate
        streams, so without this override every fast/subagent lock held
        would sit at the full 300s TTL regardless of the metadata-based fix
        above. ProxyLogging only routes a request through this hook at all
        if a callback overrides it (litellm/proxy/utils.py,
        ProxyLogging._callback_capabilities / needs_iterator_wrap check
        `"async_post_call_streaming_iterator_hook" in cls_attrs`, a leaf-
        class __dict__ check — this method must stay defined directly on
        FastSubagentLock, never on an intermediate base) — a real method
        override on this class, confirmed against the pinned tag's source
        for this PR, not merely documented. On this same pin, a client that
        disconnects mid-stream reaches this generator only as a
        GeneratorExit raised when the generator object itself is garbage
        collected (proxy_server.py), not as an immediate signal — prompt in
        practice under CPython's refcounting GC, but not a guarantee this
        file can rely on. The 300s TTL remains the hard bound for that
        case, same as every other release path here."""
        try:
            async for item in response:
                yield item
        finally:
            await self._maybe_release(request_data, user_api_key_dict)

    # Signature confirmed against LiteLLM's own docs (docs.litellm.ai/docs/
    # proxy/call_hooks, fetched for this PR) — NOT verified against a live
    # process on the pinned 1.98.0 release specifically. Still wrapped
    # defensively: a documented-but-wrong-on-this-pin signature must degrade
    # to "the lock is not released early" (falls back to the 300s TTL, the
    # pre-existing safety net), never to an exception that could interfere
    # with the actual failure response reaching the caller. Returning None
    # (rather than an HTTPException) leaves the original error untouched, as
    # documented.
    async def async_post_call_failure_hook(
        self, request_data: dict, original_exception: Exception, user_api_key_dict, traceback_str=None
    ):
        try:
            await self._maybe_release(request_data, user_api_key_dict)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - see docstring: must never break the failure path
            pass
        return None


proxy_handler_instance = FastSubagentLock()
